<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!-- PP35: the C suite's vectors, one test each.
       This project deliberately holds NO expected values. Every case it runs is parsed out of
       test/*.c at run time by the readers in the app assembly - FecVectors, CryptoVectors and the
       rest - which is the whole point: two copies of an oracle agree with each other long after
       either agrees with hardware, and that is the failure PP82 was filed for.
       What this adds over the host's selftest is granularity. The 64 recorded erasure cases are
       one assertion there and report "63 of 64" when they fail; here each is a test with the
       case's own name, so a failure says which unit count and which erasure pattern broke. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <RuntimeIdentifier>win-x64</RuntimeIdentifier>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <!-- UseWPF, because the referenced project is a WPF assembly and the test host has to resolve
         the same framework reference to load it at all.

         PP227: it is also where UI Automation comes from - UIAutomationClient and
         UIAutomationTypes, the same two assemblies PowerShell loads with Add-Type - so the harness
         that drives the real window costs no dependency at all. That matters here beyond taste:
         this port exists partly to DELETE dependencies (PP33 is two of them leaving), and a test
         harness that added one would be arguing against the thing it tests.

         PP317: and it supplies them with no item of its own. Two bare <Reference Include="…" />
         items used to say so explicitly, which asked for the assemblies down the pre-SDK path and
         printed four MSBuild warnings on every build of this tree - MSB3245 twice for not finding
         them there, then MSB3243 twice for resolving the resulting conflict "arbitrarily", which
         is a choice made by neither this project nor anybody who wrote it. Naming what the
         framework already provides is what created the second candidate to choose between. -->
    <UseWPF>true</UseWPF>
    <!-- The app's own single-file gate does not apply here: this project is never published as
         one, and its assembly has a real path on disk. -->
    <EnableSingleFileAnalyzer>false</EnableSingleFileAnalyzer>
    <!-- PP316: warnings are errors here too, and this is the project that needed it most. The two
         the compiler was printing were both about assertions - one comparing two literals, one
         reporting an empty collection without naming what was in it - so what a warning meant in
         THIS assembly was "a test says less than it looks like it says". That is the same failure
         PP56 and PP226 were filed for, arriving through the one channel already pointed at it. -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Using Include="System.IO" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.0.2" />
    <!-- PP572: a YAML reader, and it is the first package here that is not test infrastructure.
         Four classes read .github/workflows/*.yml and every one of them reads it as text -
         BuildWorkflow with regexes, GateAndCiAgree stripping comment lines and saying "Not a
         parser" in as many words. That is right for the questions they ask, and none of them asks
         whether the file is YAML at all: the runner is the first thing to read it as a document,
         and this repo has 39 red pushes and no green one to spend finding out.
         IT IS IN THIS PROJECT AND NOT IN app\. The port exists partly to delete dependencies, so
         shipping a YAML parser inside a remote-play client to gate a CI file would be arguing
         against the thing it gates. The parse lives entirely in WorkflowParseTests; app\ gains
         nothing, and `dotnet publish` of the host still resolves the three it always did.
         The alternative was a CI-side linter, which cannot run before the push it protects - and
         a hand-rolled half-parser, which PP570 has just paid for once. -->
    <PackageReference Include="YamlDotNet" Version="18.1.0" />

    <!-- WW88. The single-threaded runner, once, from a package instead of twenty-six times from
         copy-paste. Every view test here opens a WPF element on its own STA thread, because a
         control cannot be constructed off that apartment at all — so the eight lines that start
         one, bound it and report what it threw were load-bearing in twenty-six files, each with
         its own timeout and its own message.

         What that cost was not tidiness. A thread that does not finish reports NOTHING — no pass,
         no failure, no name — so the wrapper is the only thing standing between a hung UI
         primitive and a suite that says nothing at all; and the copy that gets a bound wrong is
         the one nobody notices. PP162's own comment records the shape from the other side: a
         scheduling race that was green twice and red three times on the same source.

         It also surfaces better than what it replaces. The copies rethrow as
         `XunitException(failure.ToString())`, which flattens the exception into a string — so a
         refusal a test wanted to assert on by type arrived as text. This rethrows the original,
         stack intact. Nothing here asserted on the wrapper, which is why the swap is safe: the
         one pattern that would have broken, `Assert.Throws` around the runner, appears nowhere. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Winwright.InApp" Version="0.1.0-alpha.3" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\app\ChiakiNg.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- PP162: the runner config, and it is load-bearing rather than tidy.
       xunit.runner.json turns collection parallelism OFF. Every view test opens a WPF element on
       its own STA thread, and two of those threads racing through WPF's first-touch static
       initialisation deadlock: the whole suite then reports "the STA thread did not finish" for
       every view test at once, 30 seconds apiece, while each of them passes alone. It is
       intermittent by scheduling, which is worse than broken - the suite was green twice and red
       three times on the same source.
       It costs nothing. Serial, the 1034 tests run in about a second; the parallel runs that did
       not deadlock were not measurably faster.
       Copied to the output directory explicitly. The runner reads it from beside the assembly, so
       a config that is not copied is a config that silently does not apply - which is this defect
       coming back with the file still in the tree to say it should not have. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="xunit.runner.json" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
